### What are the four patterns in the .proto file?

All four patterns are declared using the `stream` keyword on the request side, the response side, both, or neither.

```proto
syntax = "proto3";

package com.bank;

service AccountService {
  rpc GetBalance (AccountRef) returns (Balance);                       // unary
  rpc StreamStatement (AccountRef) returns (stream Transaction);       // server streaming
  rpc DepositCheques (stream Cheque) returns (DepositSummary);         // client streaming
  rpc LiveTrading (stream Order) returns (stream Fill);                // bidirectional
}

message AccountRef { string account_number = 1; }
message Balance { int64 amount_paise = 1; }
message Transaction { string txn_id = 1; int64 amount_paise = 2; string type = 3; }
message Cheque { string cheque_number = 1; int64 amount_paise = 2; }
message DepositSummary { int32 count = 1; int64 total_paise = 2; }
message Order { string symbol = 1; int64 quantity = 2; string side = 3; }
message Fill { string order_id = 1; int64 filled_quantity = 2; int64 price_paise = 3; }
```

### What is unary (one request, one response)?

Unary is the simplest and most common pattern. The client sends one message and waits for one response, exactly like a local method call. It suits ordinary request-response operations like fetching a balance or placing a single transfer.

```java
package com.bank.grpc;

import com.bank.AccountRef;
import com.bank.Balance;
import com.bank.AccountServiceGrpc;
import io.grpc.stub.StreamObserver;

public class AccountServiceImpl extends AccountServiceGrpc.AccountServiceImplBase {

    @Override
    public void getBalance(AccountRef request, StreamObserver<Balance> responseObserver) {
        long balancePaise = accountRepo.getBalancePaise(request.getAccountNumber());
        responseObserver.onNext(Balance.newBuilder().setAmountPaise(balancePaise).build());
        responseObserver.onCompleted();
    }
}
```

### What is server streaming (one request, stream of responses)?

The client makes one call and the server pushes many messages over time, then completes. It suits large result sets, live price feeds, or progress updates. The client processes messages as they arrive rather than waiting for one huge payload.

```java
@Override
public void streamStatement(AccountRef request, StreamObserver<Transaction> responseObserver) {
    List<Transaction> txns = transactionRepo.findByAccount(request.getAccountNumber());
    for (Transaction txn : txns) {
        responseObserver.onNext(txn);  // push each transaction as it is ready
    }
    responseObserver.onCompleted();    // signal end of stream
}
```

### What is client streaming (stream of requests, one response)?

The client sends a stream of requests and the server returns a single response at the end. It suits batch uploads, aggregation, or feeding many data points to compute one result.

```java
@Override
public StreamObserver<Cheque> depositCheques(StreamObserver<DepositSummary> responseObserver) {
    return new StreamObserver<>() {
        long totalPaise = 0;
        int count = 0;

        @Override
        public void onNext(Cheque cheque) {
            totalPaise += cheque.getAmountPaise();
            count++;
        }

        @Override
        public void onError(Throwable t) {
            // handle error – partial deposit may need rollback
        }

        @Override
        public void onCompleted() {
            responseObserver.onNext(DepositSummary.newBuilder()
                    .setCount(count)
                    .setTotalPaise(totalPaise)
                    .build());
            responseObserver.onCompleted();
        }
    };
}
```

### What is bidirectional streaming (both sides stream)?

Both sides send messages independently over one long-lived call. The order is not lock-stepped; each side sends at its own pace. It suits real-time interactive scenarios like live trading or chat.

```java
@Override
public StreamObserver<Order> liveTrading(StreamObserver<Fill> responseObserver) {
    return new StreamObserver<>() {
        @Override
        public void onNext(Order order) {
            // Process order and send fill back immediately
            Fill fill = matchEngine.execute(order);
            responseObserver.onNext(fill);  // server streams fills as orders arrive
        }

        @Override
        public void onError(Throwable t) {
            // Connection dropped – reconcile open orders
        }

        @Override
        public void onCompleted() {
            responseObserver.onCompleted();
        }
    };
}
```

### How do you choose the right pattern? (comparison table)

| Pattern | Request | Response | Use case |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unary | 1 | 1 | Balance query, single transfer |
| Server streaming | 1 | Many | Long statement, price feed |
| Client streaming | Many | 1 | Batch cheque deposit, file upload |
| Bidirectional | Many | Many | Live trading, chat |

### What about flow control and backpressure?

HTTP/2 provides windowing so a fast sender does not overwhelm a slow receiver. gRPC surfaces this so you can pause producing when the peer is not ready. This is critical in server streaming when pushing thousands of transactions: the server must respect the client's receive window.

```java
// Server-side flow control check before sending:
if (responseObserver instanceof ServerCallStreamObserver) {
    ServerCallStreamObserver<Transaction> obs =
            (ServerCallStreamObserver<Transaction>) responseObserver;
    obs.setOnReadyHandler(() -> {
        while (obs.isReady() && hasMore()) {
            obs.onNext(nextTransaction());
        }
    });
}
```

### What about error handling in streams?

A stream can fail partway, so both sides implement `onError`. In banking, a client-streaming cheque deposit must decide whether partial work is committed or rolled back. A bidirectional trading stream must reconcile exactly which orders were filled if the connection drops.

```java
@Override
public void onError(Throwable t) {
    if (t instanceof StatusRuntimeException sre) {
        if (sre.getStatus().getCode() == Status.Code.DEADLINE_EXCEEDED) {
            // Client timed out – rollback partial deposit
            rollbackPartialDeposit();
        }
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Choose the pattern by data shape, not habit. If the result is one value, use unary. If one input yields many outputs, use server streaming. If many inputs yield one aggregate, use client streaming. If the interaction is genuinely conversational, use bidirectional. Overusing bidirectional where unary would do adds complexity for no benefit. Forcing a streaming workload into repeated unary calls wastes the efficiency HTTP/2 streams provide.
